Q: How to check if a function is a constructor A: <p>In the old days you just had to test for functions with a prototype property:</p>
<pre><code>typeof value=='function' &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value// function with prototype property
</code></pre>
<p>But those were the days before bound and generator functions. For example the above will return <code>false</code> for constructors created with <code>Function.prototype.bind()</code> and <code>true</code> for generator functions (which can't construct per the ES7 spec).</p>
<p>So these days there are better ways, like this cross-browser solution that also works with bound and generator functions. Acknowledgement to Ryan Hanekamp for his answer which I incorporated in my answer below. This works in both modern and old ES3+ browsers including Node.js and IE:</p>
<pre><code>var isConstructor = typeof Proxy=='function'?
function(value) {// Works in modern environments that support Proxy (ES6+)
    if (typeof value=='function') {// Test for [[Construct]] internal method
        try {// Throws if `value` is not a constructor function
            var success = new new Proxy(value, {construct:Object});
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return !!success;
}: &quot;1&quot;[0]?// true for IE8+ and other ES5ish
function(value) {// Pre-ES6 JS. Tested in IE 8 thru 11 and old Safari

    // Test for bound functions and functions with a prototype property
    // That also applies to generators since they construct in pre-ES7 JS
    var is_function = typeof value=='function';

    // In IE, host objects with a prototype property can construct too.
    // So it doesn't only apply to objects where typeof value=='function'
    var has_proto_prop = value===Object(value) &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value;

    // If the function has a prototype property and is not a built-in
    // function starting with lowercase, it is probably a construtor.
    // RegExp needed since in IE: !!alert.prototype// true for host functions
    var success = is_function &amp;&amp; has_proto_prop &amp;&amp;
        !/^\s*\w+ [a-z_]\w*\(\) {\s*\[native c/.test(value);

    // Test typeof value// 'object' (host) or 'undefined' (ActiveXObject)
    // Or no prototype property means value might be a bound constructor
    if (has_proto_prop!=is_function) {
        try {
            // instanceof can be used to test for constructors because it
            // throws when used on functions with no prototype property.
            // With bound functions it uses prototype property of target.
            // False positives can occur if bound function's target isn't
            // a constructor but has a prototype property like IE's alert()
            success = {} instanceof value || true;
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return success;
}:
function(value) {// Old ES3 JS. Tested in Internet Explorer 6 &amp; 7
    var success = typeof value=='function' &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value;
    if (typeof value=='object') {
        try {
            success = new value;// test for host objects like Image()
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return !!success;
}:
isConstructor(window)//false
isConstructor(alert)//false
isConstructor(XMLHttpRequest)//true
isConstructor(Array.bind())//true
isConstructor(function myFunc(){})//true
isConstructor(function* myGenerator(){})//false
// Caveat: IE returns false-positives on bound host objects
isConstructor(isNaN.bind())//false (works in IE since isNaN isn't a host object)
isConstructor(alert.bind())//false (however in IE this returns true (false-positive))
</code></pre>

Q: Tag a remote git repository without cloning it A: <p>Here is my simple and fast script to tag a big repo without cloning.  Assuming git client works<br />
We will config an empty repo, pull tags and push the one we want back. Replace master with your branch and my-tag with your tag.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>git init &lt;REPO&gt;
cd &lt;REPO&gt;
git config remote.origin.url ssh://&lt;REMOTE URI&gt;/&lt;REPO&gt;
git fetch --tags --force --progress -- ssh://&lt;REMOTE URI&gt;/&lt;REPO&gt; +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*
git rev-parse &quot;origin/master^{commit}&quot;
  61258aa4272410ddbaedb9ccf80d664a6bec0270
git tag my-tag 61258aa4272410ddbaedb9ccf80d664a6bec0270
git push origin my-tag
</code></pre>

Q: Getting result of .Net object asynchronous method in powershell A: <p>Powershell Module NtObjectManager from Google Project Zero does have <code>Wait-AsyncTaskResult</code></p>
<p>It avoids completely blocking the thread (prevents e.g. Register-ObjectEvent -Action not working) by only waiting for 1 second at a time. And the code is short enough to just include in your script if you don't want to depend on the module:</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/googleprojectzero/sandbox-attacksurface-analysis-tools/blob/852b1586b8c4527f8be3f486801e4830220c76d0/NtObjectManager/UtilityFunctions.ps1#L207-L222" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/googleprojectzero/sandbox-attacksurface-analysis-tools/blob/852b1586b8c4527f8be3f486801e4830220c76d0/NtObjectManager/UtilityFunctions.ps1#L207-L222</a></p>
<pre><code>function Wait-AsyncTaskResult {
    Param(
        [parameter(Mandatory, Position = 0)]
        [System.Threading.Tasks.Task]$Task,
        [int]$TimeoutSec = [int]::MaxValue
    )

    while (-not $Task.Wait(1000)) {
        $TimeoutSec--
        if ($TimeoutSec -le 0) {
            return
        }
    }

    $Task.GetAwaiter().GetResult() | Write-Output
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<hr />
<p>I also did attempt to write a <code>Wait-AsyncTaskResult</code> function myself that does not just loop but acually uses a proper powershell Wait function. (Same reason as for using the above function - to keep Actions registered via Register-ObjectEvent or Register-EngineEvent working.)</p>
<p>This function also work, but is more complicated as the one from NtObjectManager:</p>
<pre><code>function Wait-AsyncTaskResult {
    Param(
        [parameter(Mandatory, Position = 0)]
        [System.Threading.Tasks.Task]$Task,
        [int]$TimeoutSec = -1
    )
    $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
    Set-StrictMode -Version 3

    $type = Add-Type -PassThru -Name TaskToPowershellEventHelper20260318 -MemberDefinition  @&quot;
public static readonly Action&lt;System.Threading.Tasks.Task, object&gt; ActionGenerateEvent = GenerateEvent;
public static void GenerateEvent(System.Threading.Tasks.Task task, object state){
    var starteArg = (object[])state;
    var runspace = (System.Management.Automation.Runspaces.Runspace)starteArg[0];
    var sourceIdentifier = (string)starteArg[1];
    runspace.Events.GenerateEvent(sourceIdentifier,task,null,null);
}
&quot;@

    $SourceIdentifier = &quot;TASK_$([guid]::NewGuid())&quot;
    $ContinuationTask = $Task.ContinueWith(
        $type::ActionGenerateEvent,
        @([runspace]::DefaultRunspace,$SourceIdentifier)
    )

    Wait-Event -SourceIdentifier $SourceIdentifier -Timeout $TimeoutSec | Out-Null
    if($Task.IsCompleted){
        #Let exceptions from continuation action bubble up
        #only happens if task finished, event didn't trigger and timeout was used
        $ContinuationTask.GetAwaiter().GetResult()
        #Return Task result
        return $Task.GetAwaiter().GetResult()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But as Wait-Event <a href="https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/blob/28a2e35f061ab45466b7c607127c85d2c1c4381c/src/Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.Utility/commands/utility/WaitEventCommand.cs#L85-L96" rel="nofollow noreferrer">also just loops</a> and is not doing any special async await magic, there is probably no advantage to doing it this way. I still wanted to post my code, just in case someone finds it useful. - Maybe if you want to wait until either a Task finishes or some event triggers?</p>

Q: My JavaScript animation stops working after html onclick A: <p>For me, everything related to information presentation should be done using only CSS.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const form_2_buttons = document.querySelector('#twoButtons');

form_2_buttons.addEventListener('click', (evt) =&gt; 
  {
  if (!evt.target.matches('button[name="btnLogin"], button[name="btnRegister"]'))
    return
    ;
  form_2_buttons.className = evt.target.name;
  })</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body {
  font-family : "Comic Sans MS", "Comic Sans", cursive;
  }
#twoButtons &gt; button
  {
  font-size        : 30px;
  font-weight      : bold;
  background-color : #7188d3;
  border           : none;
  width            : 40%;
  transition       : 0.35s;
  cursor           : pointer;
  }
#twoButtons.btnLogin &gt; button[name="btnLogin"] ,
#twoButtons.btnRegister &gt; button[name="btnRegister"]
  {
  pointer-events: none;
  }
#twoButtons:has( button[name="btnLogin"]:hover )
  {
  &gt; button[name="btnLogin"]
    {
    background-color : #4d5e96;
    color            : #dadada;
    width            : 50%;
    }
  &gt; button[name="btnRegister"]
    {
    width : 30%;
    }
  }
#twoButtons:has( button[name="btnRegister"]:hover )
  {
  &gt; button[name="btnLogin"]
    {
    width : 30%;
    }
  &gt; button[name="btnRegister"]
    {
    background-color : #4d5e96;
    color            : #dadada;
    width            : 50%;
    }
  }
#twoButtons.btnLogin::after    { content:'login'    }
#twoButtons.btnRegister::after { content:'register' }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="inside"&gt;
  &lt;form action="" id="twoButtons"&gt;
    &lt;button name="btnLogin"    type="button" &gt; Login    &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button name="btnRegister" type="button" &gt; Register &lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>tips for info (<code>defer</code> directive):</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;
  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Document&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;style.css&quot;&gt;
  &lt;script src=&quot;script.js&quot; defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;    &lt;!-- use defer --&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;div id=&quot;inside&quot;&gt;
    &lt;form action=&quot;&quot; id=&quot;twoButtons&quot;&gt;
      &lt;button name=&quot;btnLogin&quot;    type=&quot;button&quot; &gt; Login    &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button name=&quot;btnRegister&quot; type=&quot;button&quot; &gt; Register &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Resize chart.js in Bootstrap grid layout while keeping aspect ratio A: <p>The problem here is that when the window expands the chart can't grow because there's no free space in its container (the grid row); and at the same time the container doesn't resize because the content hasn't grown. Deadlock.</p>
<p>One way of fixing this is to define a fixed-height row but that leaves gaps in the layout.</p>
<p>A better solution, as shown, is to listen for the <code>window.resize</code> event and set the container height programatically. This breaks the deadlock and allows the chart to expand.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// ---------------
// no changes here
// ---------------
const myChart = new Chart(
  document.getElementById('canvas-id'), {
    type: 'bar',
    data: {
      labels: ['a', 'b', 'c'],
      datasets: [{
        data: [3, 4, 2]
      }]
    },
    options: {
      aspectRatio: 3.5,
      maintainAspectRatio: true
    }
  }
);


// -----------------------------------------------------
// add function to set container height on window resize
// -----------------------------------------------------
const chartRow = document.getElementById('row-id');
const chartCol = document.getElementById('col-id');

addEventListener('resize', (e) =&gt; {
  // calculate chart column content width
  const style = getComputedStyle(chartCol);
  const contentWidth = chartCol.clientWidth
    - parseFloat(style.paddingLeft) - parseFloat(style.paddingRight);
  const rowHeight = Math.floor(contentWidth / myChart.aspectRatio);
  chartRow.style = `min-height: ${rowHeight}px;`; // use inline style
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.8/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet" /&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/Chart.js/4.5.0/chart.umd.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!----------------------------------------&gt;
&lt;!-- add ids to chart container row/col --&gt;
&lt;!----------------------------------------&gt;
&lt;div class='container-fluid'&gt;
  &lt;div class='row' id='row-id'&gt;
    &lt;div class='col' id='col-id'&gt;
      &lt;canvas id='canvas-id'&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;!-- further columns --&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;!-- further rows --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
